<article class="env-lemma" id="09Q4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09Q4">Lemma <span data-tag="09Q4">23.8.6</span>.</a> Let $(A, \mathfrak m)$ be a Noetherian local ring. Let $\mathfrak p \subset A$ be a prime ideal. If $A$ is a complete intersection, then $A_\mathfrak p$ is a complete intersection too. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose a prime $\mathfrak q$ of $A^\wedge $ lying over $\mathfrak p$ (this is possible as $A \to A^\wedge $ is faithfully flat by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00MC" data-tag="00MC">10.97.3</a>). Then $A_\mathfrak p \to (A^\wedge )_\mathfrak q$ is a flat local ring homomorphism. Thus by Proposition <a href="/tag/09Q2" data-tag="09Q2">23.8.4</a> we see that $A_\mathfrak p$ is a complete intersection if and only if $(A^\wedge )_\mathfrak q$ is a complete intersection. Thus it suffices to prove the lemma in case $A$ is complete (this is the key step of the proof). 
    <p>
      Assume $A$ is complete. By definition we may write $A = R/(f_1, \ldots , f_ r)$ for some regular sequence $f_1, \ldots , f_ r$ in a regular local ring $R$. Let $\mathfrak q \subset R$ be the prime corresponding to $\mathfrak p$. Observe that $f_1, \ldots , f_ r \in \mathfrak q$ and that $A_\mathfrak p = R_\mathfrak q/(f_1, \ldots , f_ r)R_\mathfrak q$. Hence $A_\mathfrak p$ is a complete intersection by Lemma <a href="/tag/09Q0" data-tag="09Q0">23.8.2</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>